From: Pyra kernel work <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: ti: aess: run the AESS init sequence

Unlike 0013 to 0016 this one is written from an observed failure rather than
from reading, because 0013-0016 booted.

What they did, on 5.6.19-m4:

  omap-abe-twl6040 sound: AESS: 9 DAI links registered
  aess 401f1000.aess: ASoC: invalid header size for type 0 at offset 0x0 size 0x1a5e8.
  aess 401f1000.aess: AESS firmware is a legacy container, 17 blocks
  aess 401f1000.aess: AESS mem P 8192 C 7852 D 16384 S 21600 bytes
  aess 401f1000.aess: AESS Firmware version 9590
  omap-abe-twl6040 sound: snd-soc-dummy-dai <-> MultiMedia1 mapping ok
  omap-abe-twl6040 sound: twl6040-dl1 <-> mcpdm-abe-dl1 mapping ok
  omap-abe-twl6040 sound: twl6040-ul <-> mcpdm-abe-ul1 mapping ok
  omap-abe-twl6040 sound: twl6040-dl2 <-> mcpdm-abe-dl2 mapping ok

and then, on closing a PCM:

  PC is at aess_set_opp_processing+0x40/0xd8
  LR is at omap_aess_dai_shutdown+0x84/0xf0
  [<c09d382c>] (soc_pcm_close+0x90/0x1c0)

aess_set_opp_processing() ends in omap_aess_write_map(), which indexes
aess->fw_info.map. That array is populated by omap_aess_init_mem() from the
config blob -- and omap_aess_init_mem() sits inside an #if 0 in
omap_aess_pcm_probe(), so the map is NULL and closing a stream dereferences
it. The card is left with a PCM mutex held, after which anything touching it,
amixer and aplay included, blocks forever.

That #if 0 carries the note "failed firmware load leads to missing/wrong opp
and failed omap_aess_init_mem". It described the consequence accurately and it
is no longer true: 0014 makes the firmware load, so the block can and must
run. Leaving it disabled was survivable only while nothing bound the AESS
component into a card; 0013 changed that.

Enabling it runs what the /dev/mem experiments in device/pyra-aess-run.py
already showed working: initialise the OPP table, init_mem to build fw_info
from the config, reset_hal, load the four memories and build the scheduler
table, tick the event generator once and stop it. pm_runtime is genuinely
enabled by this point -- omap_aess_pcm_probe() runs only because the component
is now bound -- so the pm_runtime_active() test in aess_mem.h is true and the
writes actually reach the hardware instead of being dropped.

Verified to compile: `make sound/soc/ti/` exits 0 with no errors, with 0013
through 0017 applied to a pristine tree. Whether it makes sound is the next
boot's question; what it should fix is the oops and the wedged card.

--- a/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/ti/aess/omap-aess-pcm.c
@@ -524,7 +524,23 @@
 		goto out;
 	}
 
-#if 0	// failed firmware load leads to missing/wrong opp and failed omap_aess_init_mem
+	/*
+	 * This used to be #if 0'd, with the note "failed firmware load leads
+	 * to missing/wrong opp and failed omap_aess_init_mem". That was an
+	 * accurate description of the consequence and it is no longer true:
+	 * the firmware loads.
+	 *
+	 * Leaving it disabled is not harmless now that the AESS component is
+	 * bound into a card. omap_aess_init_mem() is what populates
+	 * fw_info.map from the config blob, and omap_aess_write_map() indexes
+	 * that array. Closing a PCM calls omap_aess_dai_shutdown() ->
+	 * aess_set_opp_processing() -> omap_aess_write_map(), which with a
+	 * NULL map dereferences it and oopses:
+	 *
+	 *   PC is at aess_set_opp_processing+0x40/0xd8
+	 *   LR is at omap_aess_dai_shutdown+0x84/0xf0
+	 *   ... from soc_pcm_close+0x90/0x1c0
+	 */
 	ret = aess_opp_init_initial_opp(aess);
 	if (ret < 0) {
 		dev_info(component->dev, "No OPP definition\n");
@@ -554,7 +570,7 @@
 	omap_aess_disable_irq(aess);
 
 	pm_runtime_put_sync(aess->dev);
-#endif
+
 	aess_init_debugfs(aess);
 
 out:
